Bottom line:
- 16/16 transformer stubs are passthroughs -> 100% of typed-column transforms are no-ops.
- positions.speed_mps silently stores mph (off by 2.237x).
- security_events enum columns store raw "SentryModeStateArmed" / etc. instead of normalized values.
- 11 Semitruck fields are silently unrouted.
- Compound Location/Route fields are JSON-blob'd into scalar columns.
- The hand-curated registry vs proto drift cannot be detected by the existing tests.

The defects are architectural (dual pipelines, hand-curated registry, stub-transformer dispatch),
not isolated bugs. A patch-by-patch fix touches every file in internal/telemetry/ and internal/enums/
and STILL leaves the dual-pipeline architecture intact.

=== DECISIONS ===
Restated verbatim from prompt 0000 (the source of truth for phase-42):

| # | Decision | Choice |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Approach | Full rewrite via prompt-runner. Same machinery as phase-41. |
| 2 | Source of truth for signal metadata | Vendored Tesla `vehicle_data.proto` + `go generate`. Hand-curated `SignalRegistry`, `KnownColdSignals`, `HotCatalog`, `signal_alias.go`, and `parse_*.go` are DELETED. Adding a Tesla field = re-vendor proto + re-run codegen + add a routing entry. |
| 3 | Compound types (DoorState, Location, TireLocation, Time, Doors) | Always flatten at ingest. DoorState -> 6 bool atomics, Location -> Lat+Lng, TireLocation -> 10 bool atomics. No nested maps cross the ingest boundary. |
| 4 | Backfill of historical data | None. Start fresh on deploy. |
| 5 | Resubscribe at deploy | All vehicles. Fresh Fleet Telemetry config sent on deploy day; Tesla's process-startup snapshot reseeds all subscribed signals. |
| 6 | Legacy code | Delete, no shims. Forward-only. No deprecated wrappers, no parallel old/new paths. |
| 7 | Legacy tables | 38 tables drop with `CASCADE`. Enumerated in Prompt 0078. All current data is suspect (broken pipeline wrote it). |
| 8 | Pipeline shape | Single pipeline, every value visited exactly once. `bytes -> typed Datum -> flatten -> lookup activeUnit at T -> ToSI -> atomic typed values -> router -> write`. Coverage test in Prompt 0048 asserts every Field has exactly one route. |
| 9 | Wire-format units | DYNAMIC per vehicle display preference. When a user toggles their dashboard from Miles to km (e.g., crossing into Canada), `VehicleSpeed/Odometer/TPMS/InsideTemp` start emitting in the new unit. Tesla's developer documentation claims fixed units but is known to have errors and omissions; the proto contract - `Setting*Unit` fields are streamable Field entries, not static config - is the source of truth. The cost asymmetry settles it: Design F-when-D-true silently corrupts cross-border drivers' speed/odometer/temp/pressure forever; Design D-when-F-true costs ~1 table + ~200 LOC of correct-but-unused code. |
| 9a | Mechanism | `vehicle_unit_history(vehicle_id, unit_kind, unit_value, effective_from TIMESTAMPTZ, source)` records every `Setting*Unit` change. Every unit-bearing field at time T does a point-in-time lookup before `ToSI`. |
| 9b | `ToSI` signature | `ToSI(field, rawValue, activeUnit) (float64, error)` - 3-arg pure function. activeUnit comes from the lookup. |
| 9c | Required ingest signals | All 4 `Setting*Unit` fields subscribed at `interval_seconds=1`. Treated as critical - losing one means N hours of corrupted unit-bearing data. |
| 9d | Bootstrap | `internal/tesla/client_vehicle_data.go` REST snapshot on first connect seeds `vehicle_unit_history` from `gui_settings`. Belt-and-suspenders alongside Tesla's process-startup telemetry snapshot. |
| 9e | Missing-context behavior | If a unit-bearing field arrives with no unit context (both bootstraps missed), drop value + log warning + emit metric. Never silent corruption. |
| 9f | Storage | Canonical SI everywhere (m/s, m, Pa, degC). Single conversion site at ingest. |
| 10 | Frontend Settings page Unit selectors | App-display-only. Independent of vehicle pref + ingestion. SI -> app display happens in `web/src/lib/units/`. |

=== REASONING ===
Why this is a rewrite, not a patch
----------------------------------
The audit shows 16 HIGH findings rooted in three architectural defects, not 16 isolated bugs:
  (a) Two parallel transform pipelines (NormalizeFleetUnits switch in internal/telemetry/normalize.go vs.
      HotCatalog.Transformer field in internal/telemetry/hot_catalog*.go) running on the same value with
      different outputs based on which path the dispatcher picks.
  (b) Two parallel compound expanders (Flatten() in internal/telemetry/flatten.go vs. SignalRegistry switch
      in normalize.go that JSON-marshals compound types into strings). Same field, different shape downstream
      depending on call site.
  (c) Hand-curated SignalRegistry / KnownColdSignals / HotCatalog / signal_alias.go / parse_*.go that drift
      from Tesla's vehicle_data.proto. The existing coverage test iterates the hand-curated set, so
      missing-from-registry fields pass silently. internal/telemetry/transformers_stub.go ships 16/16
      transformers as passthrough stubs (positions.speed_mps stores mph; security_events enum columns store
      raw "SentryModeStateArmed").

Patching would touch every file in internal/telemetry/ and internal/enums/ AND still leave the
dual-pipeline architecture intact. The architectural problem is that there are two pipelines at all -
no patch fixes that. The fix has to be a single-pipeline rewrite where every value is visited exactly once.

Phase-42 is INDEPENDENT of phase-41
-----------------------------------
Phase-41 audits Go-quality across the backend (style, patterns, discipline). Phase-42 deletes the
telemetry pipeline implementation and rebuilds it. The phases have ~20% overlap (phase-41 may patch files
in internal/telemetry/ that phase-42 deletes). That overlap is intentional, not a conflict:
  - Phase-41 work that lands first is harmless: phase-42 deletes those files anyway.
  - Phase-42 work that lands first is harmless: phase-41 will find the post-rewrite files clean.
  - The phases can run in either order. Prompt 0000 has no predecessor.

The 9 + 1 decisions are LOCKED
------------------------------
Implementation prompts (0001..0079) cite this prompt as the source of truth. Disagreement at
implementation time = revisit this record, not silent override. The architecture record itself lands in
ADR-004 (Prompt 0001) and the agent-instructions file `.github/instructions/tesla-pipeline.instructions.md`
(Prompt 0002). After those two prompts land, those documents become the canonical reference; until they do,
this log is authoritative.

The decisions in plain language and why each one matters
--------------------------------------------------------
- Decision 1 (full rewrite via prompt-runner): same atomic-prompt + log-driven gate machinery proven by
  phase-41. Each prompt is independently verifiable; each leaves a log; CI re-runs gates.
- Decision 2 (codegen from vendored proto): hand-curated registries demonstrably drift (11 missing
  Semitruck fields prove this). Adding a Tesla field becomes a mechanical re-vendor + go generate; the
  reflective coverage test in Prompt 0048 then enforces "every Field has exactly one route".
- Decision 3 (always flatten compound types at ingest): no nested map[string]any crosses the ingest
  boundary. DoorState becomes 6 bool atomics; Location becomes Lat+Lng; TireLocation becomes 10 bool
  atomics. Downstream consumers see only typed scalars.
- Decision 4 (no backfill of historical data): the broken pipeline wrote everything we have. mph is
  saved in m/s columns; raw enum strings in typed enum columns. There is no rescuing it; we start fresh.
- Decision 5 (resubscribe all vehicles on deploy): Tesla's process-startup snapshot reseeds every
  subscribed signal. Combined with the REST gui_settings bootstrap, this guarantees every vehicle has a
  current Setting*Unit row in vehicle_unit_history before the first unit-bearing field arrives.
- Decision 6 (delete legacy code, no shims): forward-only. No deprecated wrappers, no parallel old/new
  paths. The single-pipeline invariant cannot survive a parallel old path running alongside it.
- Decision 7 (38 legacy tables drop CASCADE in Prompt 0078): safe because (a) all current data is
  suspect and not worth saving (Decision 4), AND (b) consumer migration prompts (0060..0071) MUST land
  before 0078 so no live code references the dropped tables. The CASCADE is the safety net for any
  view/index/foreign-key we missed.
- Decision 8 (single pipeline, every value visited exactly once): the entire architectural correction.
  bytes -> typed Datum -> flatten -> lookup activeUnit at T -> ToSI -> atomic typed values -> router ->
  write. The reflective coverage test in Prompt 0048 asserts every Field has exactly one route, so a
  new proto field cannot silently fall off the pipeline.
- Decision 9 (DYNAMIC wire-format units via vehicle_unit_history + per-emission lookup):
    - The proto contract is the source of truth. Setting*Unit fields are streamable Field entries, not
      static config. Tesla's developer docs claim fixed units, but the docs are demonstrably incomplete.
    - Cost asymmetry settles the choice: Design F-when-D-true silently corrupts cross-border drivers'
      speed/odometer/temp/pressure forever; Design D-when-F-true costs ~1 table + ~200 LOC of
      correct-but-unused code.
    - Mechanism: vehicle_unit_history(vehicle_id, unit_kind, unit_value, effective_from TIMESTAMPTZ,
      source) records every Setting*Unit change. Every unit-bearing field at time T does a point-in-time
      lookup before ToSI.
    - ToSI(field, rawValue, activeUnit) (float64, error) - 3-arg pure function. activeUnit comes from
      the lookup. No globals, no implicit per-vehicle state inside ToSI.
    - All 4 Setting*Unit fields are subscribed at interval_seconds=1 because losing one means N hours
      of corrupted unit-bearing data.
    - Bootstrap from internal/tesla/client_vehicle_data.go REST snapshot seeds vehicle_unit_history
      from gui_settings on first connect. Belt-and-suspenders alongside Tesla's process-startup
      telemetry snapshot.
    - Missing-context behavior: drop value + log warning + emit metric. Never silent corruption.
    - Storage: canonical SI everywhere (m/s, m, Pa, degC). Single conversion site at ingest.
- Decision 10 (frontend Settings unit selectors are app-display-only): the app's user preference is
  independent of the vehicle's display preference and of ingestion. SI -> app display conversion lives
  in web/src/lib/units/. Backend stores SI; frontend renders in the user's chosen unit.

Single-pipeline invariant: enforced, not asked nicely
-----------------------------------------------------
Prompt 0048 contains a reflective coverage test that walks every Field in the generated proto enum and
asserts it has exactly one route through the new pipeline. A new Tesla proto field cannot become a
silent unrouted signal; the test fails the build. This is the structural protection that the
hand-curated registry never had.

Sequencing safety
-----------------
Prompts 0001..0047 build the new pipeline. Prompts 0048..0059 add the coverage test, validators,
and observability. Prompts 0060..0071 migrate every consumer from the old data sources to the new
ones. Only after consumers are migrated does Prompt 0078 DROP the 38 legacy tables with CASCADE,
and Prompt 0079 deletes the legacy internal/telemetry/ directory. The CASCADE is the last line of
defense for anything missed; the consumer migrations are the primary safety mechanism.

Forward-only, no shims
----------------------
There is no deprecated wrapper, no feature flag, no old/new flag-flip mode. The new pipeline either
works on deploy day for every vehicle (resubscribe = Tesla snapshot reseeds Setting*Unit + REST
bootstrap fills in any holes), or it doesn't ship.

